WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.440
This puffy stitch technique was shared

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.880
by Julie in our subscriber forum,
it produces a thick,

3
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.480
squishy fabric on the standard gauge
machine without any hand manipulation.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.200
It's really best worked on an automatic

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.280
patterning machine,
although you could select needles

6
00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:25.560
for every row. The stitch pattern
is simply a 1x1 pattern.

7
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.480
You could use this design or lock a
design with every other stitch being

8
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:39.080
selected. The object is to slip the same
needles throughout the pattern repeats.

9
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.440
The texture comes from slip stitch,

10
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.720
remember, with slip stitch,
the working yarn passes under the needles

11
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:53.440
being slipped. You'll slip every-
other-needle for 9 rows.

12
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.960
And then knit one row plain
across all of the needles.

13
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:10.320
Slip every other needle for 9rows and
then at one row over all of the needles.

14
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.000
Repeating this gives you raised puffy

15
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.800
ridges on the purl side
and creates a thick fabric.

16
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.240
Now you can play with the number of rows,

17
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.440
but be sure to be consistent and keep
the repeats to an even number.

18
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.880
Looking at the knit side, you can see
how every other stitch has been slipped.

19
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.000
This stitch pattern has a lot of stretch

20
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.240
width-wise,
but it's really quite stable lengthwise.

21
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:47.240
Julie used the stitch pattern
for a nice thick cover on a clothes hanger.

22
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.080
She also suggested using it for slippers.

23
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.720
Again, it's a great way to get a thick,
comfy fabric from a standard gauge machine.

24
00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:02.320
For Brother knitters set up your stitch
pattern, either electronics or punch card.

25
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.120
Set the KC knob

26
00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.920
so the machine reads the stitch pattern.

27
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.560
Push in both part buttons
and knit 9 rows.

28
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.280
The part buttons will be doing the slipping.

29
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.560
Disengage the part buttons
and knit one row plain.

30
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.280
Do it again, push in both part buttons,

31
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:31.320
knit 9 rows. Disengage the part
buttons again and knit one row.

32
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.520
And keep repeating for as long as you need
the piece. Because you're building up those

33
00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.200
raised ridges, it's going to take
a lot of rows to create any length.

34
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.640
For Silver Singer, Studio knitters,
set-aside levers to knit in pattern.

35
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:55.680
Engage your electronics or lock your punch
card to select every other needle.

36
00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.960
Set the cam lever to slip
and knit 9 rows.

37
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:06.640
Set the cam lever for plain
knitting, knit one row.

38
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.640
Cam lever to slip knit 9 rows.Cam lever
for plain knitting in one row.

39
00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.840
Repeat for the desired length.

40
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.960
Have fun with this puffy stitch technique
and be sure to share how you've used it.

